    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The calculated modal parameters are given in Table2."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 15,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 309.027, 682.07, 461.246, 693.197 ],
    "font" : "Arial-ItalicMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "3.3.3 Experimental modal analysis"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 16,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 309.027, 589.617, 538.407, 673.136 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "As said before, the calculation is only possible if we possess all the mechanical parameters but for larger shaker it is often impossible to block all the DOFs alternatively and to make it vibrate in order to extract the parameters. However, as presented in the theoretical review, the global mechanical parameters are equivalent to the modal ones."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 17,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 309.017, 517.099, 538.595, 588.526 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "In [9], Guillaume presented a method for the spectral identification of modal parameters by Least Squares Complex Frequency-Domain estimation. The LMS PolyMax method is based on this method and is recognized to be very fast and accurate for modal estimation [10]."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 18,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 309.007, 444.729, 538.456, 516.127 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "We introduced a force with an impact hammer in the two DOFs of the shaker (respectively the table and the body) and measured the acceleration of those DOFs. The LMS PolyMax method computed the transfer functions and estimated the modal parameters with the stabilization diagram presented in Figure 8."
  }, {
    "type" : "caption",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Caption",
    "id" : 19,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 319.196, 432.608, 507.38, 443.638 ],
    "linked content id" : 20,
    "font" : "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The corresponding values are given in Table 2."
  }, {
    "type" : "image",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Figure",
    "id" : 20,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 309.0, 274.64, 538.55, 423.59 ],
    "alt_source" : "missing",
    "source" : "00945e98ea0970dcffb8f548336df3a0137f_page_4_pg1_images/imageFile3.png"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 21,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 309.05, 244.313, 538.497, 265.08 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Fig. 8. Stabilization diagram of the impact testing on the shaker."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 22,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 309.046, 163.434, 538.635, 234.855 ],
    "font" : "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "When looking at these values, we may observe that the experimental values match the calculated ones for the body-related parameters, especially for the modal mass and the modal stiffness, respectively m2 and k2, and in a less manner for the modal damping c2. On the contrary, we have no match for the table-related parameters."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 23,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 309.049, 66.832, 538.627, 162.342 ],
    "font" : "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "This difference is also noticeable in Figure 8. The response curves of the body (P2-P5) clearly show a resonance, making the body-related parameters identification possible, while the table resonance, identified in the stabilization diagram to be located at 69,104Hz, is not visible (P1). We may conclude that the experimentally determined table-related parameters are not valid."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
